-I6. This is also open on both ends (F9 and J5), threatening to become an Open 4. This is a classic Double Threat – they must block both lines. This ensures my victory, because they can only block one, and I can extend the other to a 5.\n\nThis is a double threat – a classic "fork" in Gomoku. White *cannot* block both. I\'m choosing I6 because it guarantees a win.\n\nAnd it is not a forbidden (Double 3) move!\n\nThis is the winning move.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows that Black\'s major lines (the diagonal F5-I8 and the row 8 F8-I8) are blocked by White stones at the ends (E4/J9 and E8/J8), rendering them "dead" for immediate winning purposes. However, Black has a cluster of active stones around G6, H6, H7, and G8. White has no immediate threats (such as a 4-in-a-row) that require a forced response. Therefore, Black should look for a multi-threat move (a fork).\n\nPromising moves analyzed:\n1.  **H5**: Creates a vertical "four" (H5-H6-H7-H8). This forces White to block at H4. While forcing, it does not create a subsequent winning threat and fills a liberty, potentially limiting future options.\n2.  **F6**: Creates a horizontal "open three" (F6-G6-H6). While good, it only presents one threat which White can easily block.\n3.  **I6**: This move connects with two separate groups of Black stones to form two simultaneous "open three" lines:\n    *   **Horizontal:** Connects with G6 and H6 to form the line G6-H6-I6. This is open at both ends (F6 and J6).\n    *   **Diagonal:** Connects with G8 and H7 to form the line G8-H7-I6. This is open at both ends (F9 and J5).\n\nReason: The move at I6 creates a "Double Three" (two simultaneous open-3 threats). White can only block one of these lines on their next turn. Black will then be able to extend the unblocked line to an "open four" (which guarantees a win as it requires two blocking moves to stop) and subsequently a 5-in-a-row. White has no immediate counter-threats to prevent this.\n\nAnswer: {"col":